      "title": "[Advisory] Microsoft Exchange: Arbitrary Code Execution via XSS (CVE-2026-42897)",
      "content": "##### SEVERITY AND IMPACT:\r\n- Successful exploitation allows attackers to execute arbitrary code in a victim's browser and perform network spoofing via malicious emails.\r\n- **Current exploitation status**: Actively Exploited\r\n\r\n##### AFFECTED PRODUCTS:\r\n- Microsoft Exchange Server\r\n- Microsoft Exchange Server Subscription Edition (SE)\r\n- For specific versions, please check the references.\r\n\r\n##### VULNERABILITY DETAILS:\r\n- **Prerequisites:** Attackers must send a specially crafted email, and the targeted user must open it in Outlook Web Access.\r\n- **Impact Details:** Code executes in the user's browser context, which could allow attackers to bypass security boundaries, spoof content, or perform unauthorized actions.\r\n- **Available Mitigations:**\r\n    - Official full patches are currently pending or restricted to specific update programs.\r\n    - **Temporary:** Enable the automated Exchange Emergency Mitigation Service (EEMS) or run the manual Exchange on-premises Mitigation Tool (EOMT) script.\r\n\r\n##### CVEs IN THIS ADVISORY:\r\n- CVE-2026-42897 CVSS3.1: 8.1 (HIGH) - Cross-site scripting resulting in spoofing and arbitrary code execution\r\n\r\n##### REFERENCES:\r\n\r\nPrimary:\r\n- Source Advisory: https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/microsoft/microsoft-warns-of-exchange-zero-day-flaw-exploited-in-attacks/\r\n- Vendor: https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-42897\r\n\r\nSupporting:\r\n- News Coverage: https://thehackernews.com/2026/05/on-prem-microsoft-exchange-server-cve.html\r\n\r\n*Timestamp of the Information: 15.05.2026, 13:23*\r\n\r\n**Update 30.07**    \r\nProof point published a blog post detailing a campaign exploiting CVE-2026-42897:   \r\nhttps://www.proofpoint.com/us/blog/threat-insight/cleaning-out-inboxes-ta488-comes-outlook-another-half-click-exploit",
      "publicationStatus": "Open",
      "summary": "\nA high-severity security vulnerability has been identified in on-premises Microsoft Exchange Server instances that permits spoofing operations through cross-site scripting (XSS). By sending a specially crafted email, an attacker could execute arbitrary JavaScript code within a targeted user's browser via Outlook Web Access.",
      "citation_url": "https://security-hub.ncsc.admin.ch/#/posts/12577"
    },
