      "files": [],
      "title": "[Advisory] Microsoft AD CS: Domain Controller Impersonation (Certighost: CVE-2026-54121)",
      "content": "##### SEVERITY AND IMPACT:\n- Successful exploitation allows low-privileged domain users with network access to authenticate as a Domain Controller and extract critical account secrets via DCSync.\n- **Current exploitation status**: Proof of Concept Available\n\n##### AFFECTED PRODUCTS:\n- Microsoft Windows Server (Active Directory Certificate Services)\n- For specific versions, please check the references.\n\n##### VULNERABILITY DETAILS:\n- **Prerequisites:** Network access, standard domain account, an enterprise CA following a vulnerable chain path, and the default Machine template.\n- **Available Mitigations:**\n    - Vendor patches available\n    - **Temporary:** Disable the chase fallback by clearing the `EDITF_ENABLECHASECLIENTDC` flag and restarting Certificate Services.\n\n##### CVEs IN THIS ADVISORY:\n- CVE-2026-54121 CVSS3.1: 8.8 (HIGH) - Improper authorization in AD CS allowing privilege escalation\n\n##### REFERENCES:\nPrimary:\n- Technical Analysis: https://gist.github.com/H0j3n/a5ef2609b5f2944ac2390a191a534c26\n- Proof of Concept: https://github.com/aniqfakhrul/CVE-2026-54121\n- Vendor Advisory: https://msrc.microsoft.com/update-guide/vulnerability/CVE-2026-54121\n\nSupporting:\n- News Coverage: https://thehackernews.com/2026/07/certighost-exploit-lets-low-privileged.html\n\n\n*Timestamp of the Information: 27.07.2026, 13:17*",
      "publicationStatus": "Open",
      "summary": "A Proof of Concept for a high severity vulnerability dubbed \"Certighost\" has been published. The vulnerability, fixed as part of Microsoft's patch Tuesday for July 2026 allows low-privileged users to impersonate a Domain Controller. By exploiting this flaw, attackers can obtain a valid Domain Controller certificate and authenticate as the target machine to extract highly sensitive domain secrets. \n",
      "citation_url": "https://security-hub.ncsc.admin.ch/#/posts/12804"
    },
